    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Availability of effective mechanism for social contracting (at least at pilot level) in health, including service standards using social contracting frameworks</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uzbekistan has initiated pilot mechanisms for social contracting in the health sector, particularly through the State Fund for Medical Insurance (SFMI). These efforts focus on developing and implementing frameworks for contracting health services, including the establishment of service standards.
socieux.eu

A notable pilot project commenced in the Syr-Darya region in July 2021, aiming to test and refine social contracting mechanisms.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2026-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2026-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project&apos;s outcomes are intended to inform broader implementation across the country by 2025. The initiative encompasses the development of methodological guidelines for:

Defining packages of reimbursable healthcare services, including medicines.

Setting prices for these services.

Establishing agreements with service providers.

Implementing performance-based assessments to ensure efficiency and quality.

These guidelines are designed to align with international standards and EU best practices, aiming to enhance efficiency, fairness, and transparency in health service delivery. 

While comprehensive national legislation on social contracting in health is still under development, these pilot initiatives represent significant steps toward institutionalizing social contracting frameworks in Uzbekistan&apos;s healthcare system.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
